WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.393
Comparing unpaired samples of a population measured
at different points in time is a core challenge in biology,

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:10.031
where measuring cells often destroys them.

00:00:10.111 --> 00:00:14.240
Optimal transport can recover how samples map
across distributions,

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.941
but classical OT assumes mass is conserved,

00:00:17.021 --> 00:00:22.406
which breaks down when populations grow or shrink
through cell proliferation and death.

00:00:22.486 --> 00:00:24.165
This paper introduces NubOT,

00:00:24.245 --> 00:00:29.567
a neural unbalanced optimal transport method built
on the formalism of semi-couplings,

00:00:29.647 --> 00:00:34.027
letting it explicitly account for the creation and
destruction of mass.

00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:36.729
Through a cycle-consistent training scheme,

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:40.686
NubOT learns both where cells move and how their
mass rescales,

00:00:40.766 --> 00:00:45.774
and when applied to forecasting how cancer cell
lines respond to dozens of drugs,

00:00:45.854 --> 00:00:52.810
it outperforms prior neural OT methods while
recovering biologically meaningful proliferation and death signals.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:59.703
A recurring problem in the natural sciences is
modeling how a population changes after an intervention,

00:00:59.783 --> 00:01:04.503
when you can only observe unpaired snapshots rather
than track individuals.

00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:09.688
In single-cell biology this is unavoidable, because
profiling a cell destroys it,

00:01:09.768 --> 00:01:12.569
so the same cell can never be measured twice.

00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:18.137
Optimal transport offers a principled way to infer
these correspondences by learning an

00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:20.634
optimal coupling between distributions.

00:01:20.714 --> 00:01:24.346
But the standard formulation assumes conservation of mass,

00:01:24.426 --> 00:01:28.443
meaning every unit of source mass must be
transported somewhere.

00:01:28.523 --> 00:01:32.091
That assumption is violated exactly when it matters most,

00:01:32.171 --> 00:01:35.548
in unbalanced settings where cells proliferate or die,

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:39.260
and the total population size shifts between measurements.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:46.594
Cellular responses to drugs are highly
heterogeneous: different cell types and states can respond in opposite ways,

00:01:46.674 --> 00:01:48.796
some proliferating while others die.

00:01:48.876 --> 00:01:52.955
Capturing this requires nonlinear maps at the level
of single cells,

00:01:53.035 --> 00:01:54.362
not aggregate averages.

00:01:54.442 --> 00:01:57.971
Prior neural optimal transport methods can learn
such maps,

00:01:58.051 --> 00:02:02.375
but they either assume the balanced setting where
mass is conserved, or,

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.433
like the state-of-the-art unbalanced GAN approach,

00:02:05.513 --> 00:02:10.816
they only capture the general trend of growth and
shrinkage without recovering the exact

00:02:10.896 --> 00:02:13.079
reweighting each subpopulation needs.

00:02:13.159 --> 00:02:18.279
What has been missing is a method that jointly and
accurately models where mass moves

00:02:18.358 --> 00:02:20.909
and how much of it is created or destroyed,

00:02:20.989 --> 00:02:23.600
in a way that stays faithful to the biology.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:27.164
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:27.244 --> 00:02:30.428
First,

00:02:30.508 --> 00:02:33.692
it introduces a novel formulation of the unbalanced
optimal transport problem that connects

00:02:33.772 --> 00:02:36.956
the rigorous theory of semi-couplings,

00:02:37.036 --> 00:02:40.220
which allow mass to vary,

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.484
with a practical and scalable optimal-transport
mapping estimator.

00:02:43.564 --> 00:02:46.748
Second,

00:02:46.828 --> 00:02:50.012
it derives a computationally feasible
implementation based on dual potentials parameterized

00:02:50.092 --> 00:02:53.276
by input convex neural networks,

00:02:53.356 --> 00:02:56.540
together with learned reweighting functions that
predict mass changes.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.804
Third,

00:02:59.884 --> 00:03:03.068
it proposes a new cycle-consistent training
procedure that alternates between updating

00:03:03.148 --> 00:03:06.332
these maps and rescaling functions,

00:03:06.412 --> 00:03:09.596
and crucially generalizes to new, out-of-sample cells.

00:03:09.676 --> 00:03:12.860
Together these give the first neural method that
estimates semi-couplings for unbalanced OT at single-cell scale.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:18.733
NubOT's key idea is to split the hard unbalanced
problem into two coupled pieces: feature

00:03:18.813 --> 00:03:20.862
transformation and mass rescaling.

00:03:20.942 --> 00:03:26.372
It introduces proxy measures that are simply
rescaled versions of the source and target,

00:03:26.452 --> 00:03:31.507
weighted by scalar fields eta and zeta, chosen so
the two proxies have equal mass.

00:03:31.587 --> 00:03:36.704
Between these now-balanced measures it solves a
standard optimal transport problem,

00:03:36.784 --> 00:03:42.340
learning forward and backward Monge maps as the
gradients of convex potentials represented

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.343
by input convex neural networks.

00:03:44.423 --> 00:03:49.040
Because the marginals themselves shift as the
reweighting functions update,

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.613
NubOT uses an alternating minimization: it
estimates the mass rescaling with an efficient

00:03:54.693 --> 00:03:57.055
single-step unbalanced Sinkhorn update,

00:03:57.135 --> 00:04:01.187
then updates the transport potentials through a
min-max objective,

00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:02.690
cycling between the two.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.259
The reweighting functions are learned as neural networks,

00:04:06.339 --> 00:04:13.210
so at test time the model can map a new cell and
simultaneously predict whether its mass should grow or shrink.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:17.673
The method is evaluated on two settings.

00:04:17.753 --> 00:04:21.836
The first is a synthetic benchmark: a
two-dimensional mixture of three Gaussian clusters,

00:04:21.916 --> 00:04:25.999
where the target keeps the same clusters but
changes their proportions and shifts them in space,

00:04:26.079 --> 00:04:30.162
across three scenarios of increasing imbalance.

00:04:30.242 --> 00:04:34.325
The second is a real single-cell perturbation dataset,

00:04:34.405 --> 00:04:38.488
generated with the imaging technology called 4i,

00:04:38.568 --> 00:04:42.652
that tracks two co-cultured melanoma cell lines
responding to twenty-five different drugs.

00:04:42.732 --> 00:04:46.815
Cells are measured at zero, eight, and twenty-four hours,

00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:50.978
with control cells exposed only to a vehicle
serving as the source and the perturbed populations serving as targets.

00:04:51.058 --> 00:04:55.141
The two cell lines are distinguished by mutually
exclusive protein markers,

00:04:55.221 --> 00:04:59.304
MelA and Sox9,

00:04:59.384 --> 00:05:03.467
and additional markers for proliferation and cell
death allow the predicted mass changes

00:05:03.547 --> 00:05:07.630
to be checked against biology.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:12.095
Across the real single-cell task, NubOT outperforms
every baseline,

00:05:12.175 --> 00:05:15.452
including the current state-of-the-art unbalanced GAN,

00:05:15.532 --> 00:05:18.684
in almost all of the twenty-five drug perturbations,

00:05:18.764 --> 00:05:25.709
measured by a weighted version of kernel maximum
mean discrepancy between predicted and observed perturbed cells.

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:26.952
Just as importantly,

00:05:27.032 --> 00:05:32.422
it does so while getting the mass changes right: on
the synthetic benchmark it maps each

00:05:32.502 --> 00:05:36.649
cluster to its correct target without leaking mass
between clusters,

00:05:36.729 --> 00:05:40.193
and it predicts the exact reweighting each cluster needs,

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:45.228
where the GAN baseline only captures the broad
direction of growth and shrinkage.

00:05:45.308 --> 00:05:51.320
This combination of accurate feature mapping and
accurate mass rescaling is what sets NubOT apart.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:56.005
To test whether the predicted mass changes are
biologically meaningful,

00:05:56.085 --> 00:05:59.587
the authors compare them against independent measurements.

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:03.292
Summing NubOT's predicted weights within each
subpopulation,

00:06:03.372 --> 00:06:08.789
they find a strong correlation with the observed
change in cell counts after eight hours,

00:06:08.869 --> 00:06:11.877
with a correlation coefficient of point nine five.

00:06:11.957 --> 00:06:15.891
After twenty-four hours the correlation drops to
point four four,

00:06:15.971 --> 00:06:22.437
because for some drugs the induced cell death is so
severe that too few cells remain to evaluate reliably,

00:06:22.517 --> 00:06:24.537
yet the overall trend still holds.

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:29.663
Going further, the predicted weights line up
spatially with a proliferation marker,

00:06:29.743 --> 00:06:32.442
Ki67, where NubOT predicts weights above one,

00:06:32.522 --> 00:06:36.457
and with an apoptosis marker where it predicts
weights below one,

00:06:36.537 --> 00:06:41.891
confirming that the model is recovering real
proliferation and death signals rather than

00:06:41.971 --> 00:06:44.300
fitting the distribution superficially.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.132
The headline numbers tell a clear story.

00:06:47.212 --> 00:06:54.221
NubOT beats every baseline in almost all of the
twenty-five drug perturbations on the distributional fit metric.

00:06:54.301 --> 00:07:00.045
Its predicted per-subpopulation weights correlate
with observed cell counts at a coefficient

00:07:00.125 --> 00:07:02.387
of point nine five after eight hours,

00:07:02.467 --> 00:07:04.286
an extremely strong agreement,

00:07:04.366 --> 00:07:10.173
and still at point four four after twenty-four
hours despite drug-induced cell death thinning

00:07:10.253 --> 00:07:11.882
the observable populations.

00:07:11.962 --> 00:07:18.212
The evaluation spans two co-cultured melanoma cell
lines distinguished by the MelA and Sox9 markers,

00:07:18.292 --> 00:07:21.440
imaged at three time points with the 4i technology,

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:27.010
and compares against four baselines including the
prior state-of-the-art unbalanced GAN.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:33.607
The takeaway is that unbalanced optimal transport
can be made both practical and biologically faithful.

00:07:33.687 --> 00:07:36.848
By reformulating it through learnable semi-couplings,

00:07:36.928 --> 00:07:42.411
and training the maps and reweighting functions
with a cycle-consistent alternating scheme,

00:07:42.491 --> 00:07:49.503
NubOT simultaneously predicts the movement and the
creation or destruction of mass at the level of individual cells.

00:07:49.583 --> 00:07:55.311
It outperforms previous neural optimal transport
methods on the challenging task of forecasting

00:07:55.391 --> 00:07:57.696
how cancer cell lines respond to drugs,

00:07:57.776 --> 00:08:03.626
and it does so while producing predictions that
align with known proliferation and death markers.

00:08:03.706 --> 00:08:08.578
Explicitly modeling cell birth and death, rather
than assuming mass is conserved,

00:08:08.658 --> 00:08:10.290
is what makes this possible.
